      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Maybe they could include a config file so we can decide what \"Very Poor\" actually is. No offense to the devs or Tupper, but when the system equates a user with a large bounds size to a user with several thousand avatars worth of active components, making them both \"Very Poor\", that system does me no good at all. I can't block \"Very Poor\" without seeing robots amongst all sorts of people, who just went a little over one or two limits. Often, that means their avatar isn't even intensive. For example, an avatar with 10,000 mesh particle poly is NOTHING. Even a Quest can handle that, since the particle meshes aren't even skinned mesh renderers, just normal meshes. That avatar wouldn't even need to have a body, or anything else, and would be called \"Very Poor\" merely for the poly count being over 5000 on a particle or multiple particles.\n\nMeanwhile people who went insane, 20x over every limit, millions of poly on dozens of skinned meshes and particle systems with poly counts off the display, and hundreds of audio sources always active? Yeah, \"Very Poor\" as well. The system literally equates a performant Quest-capable avatar, with the most maliciously noncompliant ones you could design.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
